namespace pdg {
/**
\class PhysicsConstraint
\ingroup Physics
\brief A retained physical relationship between any two PhysicsBody owners.

Create through PhysicsBody methods, such as createPinJoint(), createSpring() or
createMotor(). The same API joins Sprite-to-Sprite, Sprite-to-Part and Part-to-Part.
Shapes are optional. A constraint never changes a Part's parent or bone binding.
Do not construct this class directly.

The factory receiver is body A; the `other` argument is B. Anchor coordinates are
local to the physical body, without artwork scaling. Relative angles and angular
speeds mean B minus A, clockwise positive, in radians and radians/second. Motors
and forces act on physical state; they do not schedule Animated operations.

Bodies retain their active constraints. Removing a body or removing/transferring
an owner from its layer disconnects them. Retained JavaScript handles remain safe
and return PhysicsBody::NoPhysics for both endpoints. A retained native handle
requires addRef()/release(). Constraints created before insertion stay dormant
until both bodies share a world. Joining two different layer worlds throws.

Basic uses bounded impulse approximations; Chipmunk uses native constraints.
Neither promises exact preservation under arbitrary loads or large timesteps.
Both support force/torque caps, break thresholds and connected-body collision
suppression. No response is applied when neither endpoint is dynamic.

\note Instantiate both PhysicsBodies first. Owner updates advance constraints;
calling PhysicsBody::step() on one detached body does not advance the other.
Native custom worlds can call PhysicsBody::solveConstraints() with both endpoints.

\code{.js}
const mount = sprite.createPart('mount');
const pendant = sprite.createPart('pendant');
mount.setupPhysicsBody().setMode(pdg.physicsBody_Static);
pendant.setLocation(0, 30);
pendant.setupPhysicsBody(1, 20);
const tether = mount.physics.createSpring(pendant.physics,
    new pdg.Point(0, 0), new pdg.Point(0, 0), 30, 20, 5);
tether.setMaxForce(100).setBreakForce(90);
\endcode

\see Collider, PhysicsBody
*/
}
